/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */

 /**
     * Approach: the idea is take root from postorder[]. search that root inside inorder[]. all the elements to the left in inorder[] will be left subtree and right elements will be right subtree. Do this recursively.
     - In order to search root element in inorder[] we are using hashmap.
    */

    /*Time Complexity: O(N)
    HashMap Construction: We iterate through the inorder array of size N exactly once to populate the HashMap. This takes O(N) time.
    
    Tree Construction (Recursion): Eecursive helper function processes every node exactly once (tracked by the global idx moving from  N-1 to 0). Inside the function, looking up the index in the HashMap and creating a new node both take O(1) time. Therefore, building the tree takes O(N) time.
    
    Total Time: O(N) + O(N) = O(N).
    
    */

    /*
    Space Complexity: O(N)
    - The HashMap: Storing all N nodes and their indices takes O(N) space.
    - The Call Stack (Implicit Space): Because this is a recursive solution, the system uses the call stack to   manage the function calls. The maximum depth of this stack is equal to the height of the tree (H).
    - In the worst-case scenario (a completely skewed tree, like a linked list), the call stack will go N levels deep, taking O(N) space.
    - In the best-case scenario (a perfectly balanced tree), the call stack takes O(log N) space.
    */
class Solution {
    //this index is on postorder array;
    int idx;
    Map<Integer, Integer> map;

    //inorder -> left- root-right.
    //postorder -> left- right- root.
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] inorder, int[] postorder) {
        this.idx = postorder.length - 1;
        map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < inorder.length; i++) {
            map.put(inorder[i], i);
        }

        return helper(0, inorder.length - 1, postorder);
    }

    private TreeNode helper(int inStart, int inEnd, int[] postorder) {
        //1. base
        if (inStart > inEnd)
            return null;

        //2. logic
        int rootVal = postorder[idx];
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(rootVal);
        idx--;

        //3. recurse
        int rootIdx = map.get(rootVal);

        //create right subtree
        root.right = helper(rootIdx + 1, inEnd, postorder);

        //create Left subtree
        root.left = helper(inStart, rootIdx - 1, postorder);
        return root;

    }
}